# 6. CYBERSECURITY & HARDENING AUDIT

Create an actual external security posture assessment.

Inspect the following where technically observable.

## 6.1 HTTPS and TLS

Check:
- Whether HTTPS is available.
- Whether HTTP redirects to HTTPS.
- TLS connection availability.
- Certificate validity.
- Certificate expiry information when available.
- Certificate hostname match.
- Certificate issuer.
- Certificate chain verification.
- Observable protocol negotiation.
- Mixed-content references in scanned HTML.

Do not claim that an unavailable TLS check passed.

## 6.2 HTTP security headers

Inspect:

- Strict-Transport-Security
- Content-Security-Policy
- X-Content-Type-Options
- X-Frame-Options
- Referrer-Policy
- Permissions-Policy
- Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy
- Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy
- Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy

For each finding, provide:

- Header name.
- Actual observed value.
- Whether it is missing, weak, or appropriately configured.
- Security impact.
- Severity.
- Recommended configuration.
- Fix example.
- Verification instructions.

Do not treat every missing header as a confirmed exploitable vulnerability.

Evaluate the context.

## 6.3 Cookie security

Inspect publicly observable Set-Cookie response attributes.

Check:
- Secure.
- HttpOnly.
- SameSite.
- Cookie scope.
- Expiration attributes.

Never display session cookie values or authentication secrets in reports.

## 6.4 DNS security signals

Where supported, inspect:
- DNS resolution.
- A and AAAA records.
- CNAME.
- MX records.
- TXT.
- SPF.
- DMARC.
- CAA.
- DNSSEC availability.

Separate DNS observations from verified configuration conclusions.

## 6.5 Other external security observations

Assess:
- Publicly exposed security.txt.
- Publicly discoverable version banners.
- HTTP redirect behaviour.
- Information disclosure in response headers.
- Public robots.txt configuration.
- Public sitemap availability.
- Unexpected server error responses.

Do not perform exploitation or destructive tests.

Do not run brute-force attempts, credential attacks, injection payloads, denial-of-service tests, authentication bypass attempts, or intrusive endpoint discovery.

OriginSec is an authorized defensive auditing platform.

